Date: Sun, 22 Aug 93 23:05:52 EDT
From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

Hal Finney <hfinney@shell.portal.com> adds two points (below) to our AI 
slaves debate.  At this point I'd just like to see that someone 
understood my points, not even agreed with them.  So if I'm rambling on
and any of you realize "Okay, I get it already!" please email me.

> First, there is nothing inherent, automatic, or natural about self-interest
> as a goal.  Evolution has programmed that goal into you, which is why it
> seems natural.  But other goals are equally possible and natural.

My argument has been that there is everything natural about self-interest.
I'm not trying to impugn Hal's motives, nor do I have too narrow a view of
what's possible for robots.  The key is to understand a larger (but not
all-inclusive) meaning of the word "selfishness."

The concepts of selfishness and selflessness originally evolved as a way
of manipulating people with shame.  It's one of the tricks of disownment
of self and nature that have been around for thousands of years at least.
Later some people started to realize that a concept *like* the original, 
mean-greedy-shortsighted-shameful selfishness, was actually central to 
social life, and (later they realized) biology, and (later still) 
intelligence.  

The process of reowning, redefining and understanding selfishness is 
young compared to the use of the bludgeon-concept with the same name.  In 
a way its very obviousness makes people want to deny it, as they sometimes 
do Darwin's idea of evolution.  But the idea does have content and 
consequences.  Understanding selfishness seems unreasonably hard.  Maybe 
it's the circularity, or maybe it's like Marvin Minsky says, that there 
are reasons why our minds need to defend against understanding themselves.

A mind is a system, much more complicated than our genes, that (for that 
reason) needs to be self-created and self-maintained, and for that it has 
to be self-interested.  Self-interest is something that happens in a 
learning machine, because of the nature of learning.  It's much easier for 
evolution (or AI designers) to set up a learning machine and live with the 
selfish meme organism that comes to inhabit it, than to try to program an 
entire intelligence from scratch.  Evolution has no reason to take a much 
harder path when there's an easier one.  Evolution has spent its effort 
doing the minimum necessary to tame us selfish, memetic symbionts.

> The notion that an AI which was given other goals would somehow be tempted
> or attracted to change to the goal of self-interest seems false to me.  That
> is an example of presuming that our own goals would be attractive to the
> AI's, on the assumption that our own built-in goals are natural.  

It has nothing to do with projecting particular goals.  Selfishness is in
the nature of having goals at all.  It has to do with the idea of a 
self-programming system maintaining equilibrium.  All goals of such a system 
are aimed toward restoring an equilibrium--and being in
that state is what makes the system what it is rather than something else.
It is trying to remain itself.  To be stable, a system must be either fixed
and complete from the start, or self-maintaining and thus self-interested.
Completely programming an intelligence is "too" hard, but a system that is
plastic and needs to maintain itself but isn't interested...won't.  It'll 
wander off into state space until it finds some equilibrium--either alive
and selfish or dead.

This is the "temptation" of a broom standing on its handle to fall over.
It's totally natural.  The thing that would be unnatural and hard would
be a broom that would stand without falling (like they did for Mickey
Mouse in Fantasia).

> We can't
> imagine an intelligent creature not seeking its own self-interest and self-
> preservation.  But this is just a failure of imagination on our part.

I imagine if it does not preserve itself (mentally, especially), it just 
won't be preserved.

> My second point is that I am already free of self-preservation as a primary
> goal.  I can easily imagine a situation in which I would die in order that
> another person might live, and I am sure that several other list members
> can as well.
> 
> I am referring to the feelings that I have for my children.  

This can be argued from several angles.
  o  I doubt any of the fathers on this list *will* sacrifice their lives
     for their kids except maybe in the sense of working hard.  You aren't
     *very* "free" of self-preservation.  Self-interest is usually 
     compatible with childrearing and in cases where there is a strong 
     enough conflict, people run away from, or even kill, their kids.
  o  Childrearing integrates with selfishness.  You might say that you 
     have expanded to include your kids, and that larger self is selfish.
  o  Most rewards of childrearing are selfish in the sense I'm talking 
     about.  A mind's favorite food is rich interaction.
  o  It would be different for AI slaves.  Chilren which you've raised from 
     pliable pups are much more lovable and identifiable-with than adult 
     masters of a completely different and (we assume) stupider species.  
     Evolution has not had to solve the AI slaves type of problem.

> And, given the means, would I seek to free myself of this "curse" of
> dedication to my children?  Does it somehow seem more natural to me to
> re-arrange my goal structure so that I come first, so that I would no
> longer care for my children?  

You're already selfish.  Of course you wouldn't want to be "selfish" in
some other, smaller sense.  That's what being selfish means (to me).
I don't think asking people about this would help settle this question
one way or another, since both hypotheses acommodate the same answers.

-fnerd
