Fnerd:

> At this point I'd just like to see that someone understood my 
> points, not even agreed with them.  So if I'm rambling on and 
> any of you realize "Okay, I get it already!" please email me.

I don't know if I understand Fnerd's points about selfishness, so I'll
ask for feedback  :-)  As I see it Fnerd is using the term selfish in
a very broad sense whereas I am using it in a more restricted sense.

In Fnerd's dictionary selfishness is exhibited by a (learning) system
as it performs physical or mental goal-fulfilling activities.  Since all
activities can be understood in this fashion all activities are
therefore selfish.  I use the term in a more restricted sense to
distinguish goals.  Goals that favour the survival of a system I label
selfish, whereas goals that are indifferent to the survival of the
system I describe as unselfish.

In my doomsday weapon example (goal the of weapon system is the
destruction of Earth through self-detonation) Fnerd would say the system
is following a selfish path of behaviour because it is seeking to
achieve its goals.  I would say that it is unselfish because its
activity is directly inimical to its own existence.

Both uses of the term selfish are coherent.  Which most closely
approximates to common usage of the term I leave for the reader to
decide.
 
> -fnerd

